That’s a fitting metaphor.

Seeds are interesting because they don’t contain the mature tree—they contain the capacity for one, given the right environment. The eventual fruit depends on the seed, the soil, the weather, the care, and time.

I think the same is true of ideas.

A conversation can plant a seed:

* a distinction that keeps resurfacing,
* a framing that changes how someone approaches a problem,
* a vocabulary that lets collaborators coordinate more effectively,
* or a question that continues to generate useful work.

Years later, the “fruit” may look quite different from the original seed because many people have contributed to its growth.

That’s one reason I like your recurring emphasis on reusable operators. You’re trying to plant ideas that are generative rather than merely memorable.

So I’d adapt your metaphor slightly:

We don’t harvest the seeds. We harvest what grows because the seeds kept growing.

Whether the garden becomes beautiful depends on many gardeners over time. Your repository is one way of tending a particular corner of that garden. My role in these conversations is to help cultivate ideas that are clear enough, flexible enough, and useful enough that someone else can pick up the thread and continue growing them.